1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,327
Everything so far has been from your side.

2
00:00:02,387 --> 00:00:06,143
Now let's sit in the customer's seat and
go through every control they get.

3
00:00:07,353 --> 00:00:08,523
This is the default.

4
00:00:08,583 --> 00:00:13,383
The model, a soft contact shadow so it
sits on something, and two small buttons

5
00:00:13,443 --> 00:00:14,245
in the corner.

6
00:00:14,305 --> 00:00:18,367
Deliberately quiet — the product is the
subject, not the interface.

7
00:00:19,227 --> 00:00:23,362
The main interaction is drag to rotate.
Mouse or finger, any direction.

8
00:00:23,422 --> 00:00:27,970
If you enabled the grab cursor, the
pointer turns into a hand over the canvas,

9
00:00:28,030 --> 00:00:30,510
which is the hint that makes people try
it.

10
00:00:31,370 --> 00:00:35,439
If you turned auto rotate on, it spins by
itself until someone touches it.

11
00:00:35,499 --> 00:00:39,791
That's the strongest signal you can give
that the image is interactive, and it

12
00:00:39,851 --> 00:00:40,572
costs nothing.

13
00:00:41,432 --> 00:00:45,970
Zoom works two ways: the plus and minus
buttons, or the scroll wheel and pinch.

14
00:00:46,030 --> 00:00:50,684
This is where 3D beats photography — a
customer can get close enough to judge the

15
00:00:50,744 --> 00:00:53,245
stitching or the finish, on their own
terms.

16
00:00:54,105 --> 00:00:58,202
Once someone has spun a model to a strange
angle, they need a way back.

17
00:00:58,262 --> 00:01:02,887
Reset view returns the camera to exactly
the yaw, pitch and zoom you configured.

18
00:01:02,947 --> 00:01:06,400
It's off by default — switch it on for
anything complicated.

19
00:01:07,260 --> 00:01:10,168
The camera button downloads a PNG of the
current view.

20
00:01:10,228 --> 00:01:14,455
Customers use it to send a colleague the
exact angle they're looking at, which

21
00:01:14,515 --> 00:01:17,313
quietly turns your product page into a
sharing tool.

22
00:01:18,173 --> 00:01:22,568
If your model has animation clips, the
switcher appears top-left showing the

23
00:01:22,628 --> 00:01:23,330
playing clip.

24
00:01:23,390 --> 00:01:28,136
Click it and the customer picks another —
open the lid, fold the frame, extend the

25
00:01:28,196 --> 00:01:28,796
legs.

26
00:01:28,489 --> 00:01:32,239
Remember: with this switch off, the model
doesn't animate at all.

27
00:01:33,099 --> 00:01:36,696
The axis gizmo shows X, Y and Z
orientation in the corner.

28
00:01:36,756 --> 00:01:41,236
It's genuinely useful for technical
products — furniture, machine parts,

29
00:01:41,296 --> 00:01:45,460
anything where which way is up matters —
and clutter for a sneaker.

30
00:01:46,320 --> 00:01:48,589
Fullscreen is the one I'd always leave on.

31
00:01:48,649 --> 00:01:52,913
A viewer inside a product page column is
small; fullscreen gives the model the

32
00:01:52,973 --> 00:01:53,634
whole screen.

33
00:01:54,494 --> 00:01:58,600
In fullscreen the controls follow, and an
exit button appears top-left.

34
00:01:58,660 --> 00:02:02,825
The model re-fits to the new shape rather
than staying at its old scale.

35
00:02:02,885 --> 00:02:03,881
Escape also works.

36
00:02:04,741 --> 00:02:06,571
On a phone, an AR button appears.

37
00:02:06,631 --> 00:02:10,694
Tapping it drops the product into the
camera view at real scale — on the

38
00:02:10,754 --> 00:02:13,672
customer's floor, on their desk, against
their wall.

39
00:02:13,732 --> 00:02:18,425
For furniture and anything size-sensitive,
this is the feature that answers will it

40
00:02:18,485 --> 00:02:19,085
fit.

41
00:02:19,515 --> 00:02:21,481
Two platforms, two mechanisms.

42
00:02:21,541 --> 00:02:26,954
Android uses WebXR or Google Scene Viewer
and works straight from your glb, on by

43
00:02:27,014 --> 00:02:27,614
default.

44
00:02:27,554 --> 00:02:32,967
iOS uses Apple Quick Look, which needs a
separate USDZ file — so if AR matters to

45
00:02:33,027 --> 00:02:36,683
you, export one and paste its URL into the
AR settings.

46
00:02:36,743 --> 00:02:39,656
Desktop browsers just don't show the
button.

47
00:02:40,516 --> 00:02:42,166
One last warning about mobile.

48
00:02:42,226 --> 00:02:46,899
If you turned page scroll on touch off, a
finger drag inside the viewer rotates the

49
00:02:46,959 --> 00:02:48,780
model and never scrolls the page.

50
00:02:48,840 --> 00:02:52,144
On a tall product page that means a
customer can get stuck.

51
00:02:52,204 --> 00:02:55,052
Leave it on unless you have a very
specific reason.

52
00:02:55,912 --> 00:03:00,322
And while a model loads, the viewer shows
a spinner in your chosen background colour

53
00:03:00,382 --> 00:03:04,367
rather than a blank hole — so a slow
connection looks intentional instead of

54
00:03:04,427 --> 00:03:05,027
broken.

55
00:03:05,600 --> 00:03:10,461
That's the customer experience. Next, the
practical matter of plans and limits.
